# A rule that refutes its own absence test
#
# Russell and Wittgenstein teach philosophy. One rule relates each course to
# its teachers while the current graph contains no unicorn, and another rule
# introduces a unicorn from that relation. The unicorn contradicts the absence
# the first rule assumed, so eyeron trips an inference fuse and returns no
# conclusions. The example shows that scoped negation over the current graph
# is checked against what inference derives afterwards.

@prefix log: <http://www.w3.org/2000/10/swap/log#> .
@prefix : <https://example.org/course#> .

# Facts
:philosophy :teacher :russell .
:philosophy :teacher :wittgenstein .

# A teacher is related to the course while no unicorn is known.
{
    ?course :teacher ?person .
    ?scope log:notIncludes {
        ?x a :Unicorn .
    } .
} => {
    ?course :something ?person .
} .

# That relation brings some unicorn into being.
{
    ?course :something ?person .
} => {
    _:x a :Unicorn .
} .
